00:00:00.399 --> 00:00:02.230 align:start position:0%
 
welcome back the labour party has been  embroiled in  yet another row about anti-semitism  after the bbc's panorama program made  allegations  that the leader's office had interfered  in disciplinary cases  well one of the party's most senior  jewish mps day market heads joins us now  from westminster mr very good morning to  you  good morning to you you will of course  uh have seen the bbc panera  panorama documentary and indeed the  response from the labour party  itself what were your thoughts on  hearing that first person testimony from  former labour staffers i mean to be  honest nile it's hard to express what  i'm  feeling because i was of course  devastated by it  but i don't know how often how many  people  how many incidents of anti-semitism we  have to  have on our television screens or in our  newspapers  for the party leadership to stand up  and really listen there seems to be a  dogged determination  not to listen and i think my main  feeling out of that program  when i watched it was how young  everybody was  these are young committed idealistic  labour party activists and labour party  workers  and they have been crushed by what has  happened to them  and the final thing i felt was it was  utterly deplorable  and a complete abuse of power for the  leadership's reaction  simply to to try and pursue these people  uh with lawyers and trying to uh to shut  them up  i used to chair the public accounts  committee  and um in those days whistleblowers  were really important to revealing all  sorts of stuff to us and i well remember  when we had the luxembourg leaks about  tax avoidance  or the falciana leaks about tax  avoidance in switzerland  both price pricewaterhousecoopers and  hsbc  responding by taking action against the  whistleblowers  trying to shut them up i never thought  that my labour party  would do the same about whistleblowers  trying to expose  and deal with racism in the labour party  and the response from the labour party  specifically about those  those whistleblowers as you describe  them is to call them  disaffected staffers with political axes  to grind well  i think anybody who watched that program  would have seen that they are committed  honest people with integrity who care  um about the labour party and out if the  fact that three of them  in that program had been driven into  mental health conditions  one had had a break down one had  suffered from depression  and one had contemplated suicide  for the labor party hierarchy for the  leadership of the labour party  to dismiss that as disaffected workers  i think is intolerable and unacceptable  it's a real  abuse of power and i think the labour  party should really reflect and think  again  stop this dogged determination to ignore  stop thinking they've got everything  right and they and they can  uh they don't have to listen to the rest  of us stop being blinkered about it and  pursuing the project at the expense of  really  fighting racism within the party i mean  the labour party itself is pretty  categorical about this they've doubled  the number of staffers that are working  on complaints actually when you analyze  the number of members that this refers  to it's an  infinitively small percentage of the  overall party  that some of these allegations have been  politicized i mean what more could the  party do  say that last but again i said and some  of the allegations have been publicized  what more then given the doubling of the  staffers the tiny fraction of labour  members this  uh references but what more can the  party actually do  now it's about a year since i first  confronted jeremy corbyn about the issue  in the lobby of the house of commons  coming up to the university of year and  the terrible truth is that  in that year things have got worse and i  can tell you from my own social media  you can see from people coming that in  the papers today you have another  whistleblower  you have small cases in the papers day  after day after day  more cases are coming out what can the  labour party do  well it's very easy i think the first  thing i would do  is adopt the alisa campbell uh  code of disa discipline disciplinary  code the alistar campbell disciplinary  code let's call it that  so that if there is a clear instance of  um anti-semitism and a lot of these are  absolutely blatant  and obvious if that happens then you  immediately  expel and the individual would have the  right of appeal that's the first thing i  do  the second thing that i would do is  immediately adopt  a totally independent complaints  mechanism  because it is clear that the current  mechanism  has been abused it's been corrupted by  political influence  from the leader's office and from the  leadership of the labour party  and it no longer holds good third i  would stop this action against the  whistleblowers  that is outrageous we cannot have that  and fourth i will do what tom watson has  been calling for  we're in a really serious position this  is deeply damaging to labour's prospects  we're being investigated by the ehrc  on the grounds of anti-semitism and none  of us know  what the party is saying on all our  behalfs  to the uh equal and the the  the commission so i would publish that  in the way that tom watson has  been asking us to so that we have real  openness instead of secrecy  uh we don't we have that and we  transform that culture  which at the moment i think is so deeply  damaging to labour party  we used to be a tolerant party we've  become an intolerant party  we used to be open and democratic we've  now become  secretive and we've become intimidating  we used to be anti-racist  and we are now allowing a culture of  racism  sorry to interrupt on that point i mean  you mentioned the encounter that you had  with the leader of your party you know  called him an anti-semite and a racist i  mean do you stand by that  well to be honest i've seen nothing in  the past year that has caused me  to change my mind both in the incidence  of anti-semitism that have emerged  and in the further details that we have  of  uh of jeremy corbyn himself the sort of  people he's met  the fact that uh uh that um he went to a  wreath laying  with some terrorists from terrorists but  you're still in the party i mean and if  there  were to be a snap election called in the  next few weeks you presumably would be  campaigning  for the labour party for the man that  you described as an anti-semite and a  racist  to be the next prime minister listen  i've been a member of the labour party  for 57 years it seems a heck of a long  time  and for 54 of those 57 years  the party has been one of the great most  important things in my life  a love of my life and i've had arguments  in it  i've promoted it i've worked with it to  pursue the  uh the values that i have you don't give  up on a relationship  just because there have been three or  four years now of a period  which i cannot support and i have felt  hugely uncomfortable with  i do think we are at a tipping point i  do  think that if the leadership doesn't  start to listen  now there will be many more people who  will feel  so uncomfortable within the labour party  that uh  that they can no longer remain within i  myself  just feel that it's like a marriage  you've been there  i've been there for 57 years 54 years of  complete happiness  i'm going to fight to be shipped before  margaret i mean there are lots of people  who would say if you discovered that  your husband was an anti-semite and a  racist that might be grounds to divorce  what i hope to do is to ensure that you  go  back to the values that brought me into  the labour party when i first joined  i'm myself and an immigrant and that has  been a huge im you know  formative influence on my politics and  my values  so i joined the party because it was an  anti-racist party  i joined the party because it was a  tolerant party i  joined the party because it had an  international uh  solidarity which part of its uh values  and i'm gonna fight for those values i  think you stay  you you try and bring that party back to  the values  not only for me but actually for my  constituents  who depend on everything the labour  party stands for  for their quality of life and for  greater equality  uh uh and greater fairness in our  society  i wonder if i might ask you about your  colleague tom watson um a number of  people have  complained about his behavior this week  was it right for tom watson  uh to criticize jenny formby while she  was undergoing  chemotherapy we know what lynn  mccluskey's views are and are in all of  this and so we could keep it clean  given that it's not yet 10 o'clock in a  sunday morning  um i listen we all want jen we all wish  jenny fornby well  it's terrible to have to undergo this  treatment um i  you know i had a year caring for my  husband when he was going through cancer  as well  so i know exactly what she's uh going  through  but i have to say that the part it's a  you know there are more people  responsible than jenny formby  uh there's the whole of the national  executive  there's the leader of the party there's  a shadow cabinet it's regretful that  there isn't a member of the shadow  cabinet on your  program today to answer some of these um  issues  it's a butt doesn't stop with one person  and i actually applaud  tom watson he's been very he's been  calling this out for a long time  he i think and i share this view that we  are fighting for the soul  of the labour party and i'd just like to  say one final thing i'm writing to the  labour party  all the time about incidents of  anti-semitism  and when jenny was first ill i i didn't  write to her  i wrote somebody else and i got a  response to that letter saying the  matter has been referred  to jenny formby so you cannot you know  it's very difficult in this situation  and i hope that others will now take the  lead and  leave her to uh to you know cope  with the treatment that she's undergoing  but you cannot  fail to deal with this issue and tom  watson is  absolutely right to be calling this out  this is extremely serious  and it goes to the heart of what the  labour party stands for  and the values that uh it promotes do  you think then  that the labour party is structurally  institutionally  anti-semitic now i think  that there is a culture within the  labour party that has  created a tolerance and a  a tolerance of this form of racism  that is now spread through you see too  much of it  you see it in too high you know whether  you talk about chris williams and  whether you talk about pete wilson  whether you talk about the checks that  were done or not done  on our candidate in the peterborough  by-election it is  a embedded culture at the top but what i  also know  and see is thousands upon thousands of  labour party members  who share the values that i'm talking to  you about this morning  who abhor the jew hate  that has now uh been allowed  to sort of come from the fringes into  the mainstream  of the party so i i think that  the labour party it's most people in the  labour party  abhor what is being allowed to happen  what has been given permission to exist  in that in the leadership uh cohort of  the people  at the moment running the labour party  dave margaret hodge many thanks for  joining us thank you  of course we did contact the labour  party and invited them to respond  to the latest allegations they sent us a  statement which says the  following the labour party takes all  complaints of anti-semitism extremely  seriously  and we are committed to challenging and  campaigning against it  in all its forms all complaints about  anti-semitism are  fully investigated in line with our  rules and procedures  and any appropriate disciplinary action  is taken  the rate at which anti-semitism cases  are dealt with has increased  more than fourfold since jenny formby  became general secretary that statement  from  the labour party

